# Project: Robot Framework Test Automation

## Framework Structure
- Use Robot Framework as the primary keyword-driven test runner
- Organize test suites by feature or application module
- Separate resource files, keywords, and test data from test cases
- Use Python for custom keyword libraries when built-in keywords are insufficient

## File Organization
- Place test suites in a `tests/` directory, mirroring the application structure
- Store reusable keywords and variables in `resources/` (e.g., `resources/keywords/`, `resources/variables/`)
- Keep page object resource files in `resources/pages/`
- Put custom Python libraries in `libraries/`
- Use `__init__.robot` files to define suite-level setup/teardown

## Naming Conventions
- Write test case names as readable sentences: `User Can Log In With Valid Credentials`
- Name keywords as imperative verb phrases: `Click Submit Button`, `Verify Error Message Is Visible`
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for variables: `${BASE_URL}`, `${TIMEOUT}`
- Name resource files after the feature or page they describe: `login_page.resource`, `common.resource`

## Test Organization
- Group related tests in the same suite file
- Use tags to classify tests: `[Tags]    smoke    regression    login`
- Implement suite-level `Suite Setup` / `Suite Teardown` for browser or session management
- Use `Test Setup` / `Test Teardown` for per-test initialization and cleanup

## Keyword Design
- Write keywords at a single level of abstraction; avoid mixing high-level and low-level steps
- Keep keywords short and focused — one action or verification per keyword
- Use keyword arguments with defaults for flexibility: `Open Browser To Page    url=${HOME_URL}`
- Document keywords with a single line explaining intent, not implementation

## Best Practices
- Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert (Given-When-Then) structure for BDD-style tests
- Avoid hardcoding locators in test cases; define them as variables in resource files
- Use SeleniumLibrary or Playwright Library (robotframework-browser) for web automation
- Implement explicit waits (`Wait Until Element Is Visible`) instead of `Sleep`
- Run tests in parallel with `pabot` (Parallel Robot Framework executor)

## Variable Management
- Define environment-specific variables in `.env` files and load them via a custom listener or `dotenv`
- Use Variable Files (`.py` or `.yaml`) for complex or computed test data
- Prefer `${VAR}` scalar variables for single values and `@{LIST}` / `&{DICT}` for collections
- Keep secrets out of version control; pass them via environment variables or a vault

## Page Object Pattern
- Model each page or component as a `.resource` file exposing high-level keywords
- Store locators as variables at the top of the resource file
- Never access locators directly from test cases — always go through a page keyword
- Example structure:
  ```
  *** Variables ***
  ${LOGIN_USERNAME_FIELD}    id:username
  ${LOGIN_PASSWORD_FIELD}    id:password
  ${LOGIN_SUBMIT_BUTTON}     css:button[type='submit']

  *** Keywords ***
  Log In As
      [Arguments]    ${username}    ${password}
      Input Text     ${LOGIN_USERNAME_FIELD}    ${username}
      Input Text     ${LOGIN_PASSWORD_FIELD}    ${password}
      Click Button   ${LOGIN_SUBMIT_BUTTON}
  ```

## Error Handling
- Use `Run Keyword And Expect Error` to assert that keywords raise expected failures
- Implement `Run Keyword And Continue On Failure` when multiple independent assertions are needed
- Add screenshot capture on failure via SeleniumLibrary's built-in `register_keyword_to_run_on_failure`
- Log meaningful context with `Log` and `Log To Console` before critical steps

## Test Data Management
- Store test data in CSV or YAML files and load them with `DataDriver` library for data-driven tests
- Use `Variables` section in suite files only for suite-scoped constants
- Avoid mutable global state between tests — reset state in `Test Teardown`

## Reporting
- Use the built-in `log.html` and `report.html` for local analysis
- Integrate with Allure using `robotframework-allure` listener for richer CI reports
- Attach screenshots and page source to failing tests automatically
- Configure `--outputdir results/` to keep CI artifacts organized

## CI/CD Integration
- Run Robot Framework with `robot --outputdir results --loglevel DEBUG tests/`
- Merge parallel results with `rebot --merge results/*.xml`
- Set exit codes correctly: RF returns non-zero on any test failure
- Cache `pip` dependencies and the browser binary between CI runs

## Linting and Quality
- Use `robocop` for static analysis of `.robot` files
- Use `robotidy` for automatic formatting
- Enforce a maximum keyword length (recommended: 20 lines) via robocop rules
- Run `robocop` and `robotidy --check` in pre-commit hooks

## Documentation
- Add a `[Documentation]` tag to every test case describing the business scenario
- Document custom Python libraries with docstrings; RF exposes them in `libdoc`
- Maintain a `README.md` covering setup, how to run tests, and how to add new suites

Remember to keep test cases readable by non-technical stakeholders while keeping implementation details inside keywords and resource files.
